+# Full readable reconstruction — S5-CLM-00003489
+
+This document gives a ten-node reconstruction of the exact checked proof. The
+source record supplies the proposition only; its unresolved body is not used.
+Every node below has a unique delimited fragment, names its formal evidence,
+states its inputs and output, and identifies its downstream consumers.
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:BEGIN -->
+## PU-00 — Freeze the source proposition
+
+Hypotheses: the frozen workset record and, inside the proposition,
+`L : NormalModalLogic` and
+`H : ∀ φ, proves L (t φ) ↔ proves KT φ`.
+
+`Statement.lean` reconstructs the four source formula constructors, the
+implication/falsum definition of conjunction, the recursive translation `t`,
+the source `KProof` and `KTProof` inductives, and the three-field normal-logic
+record. `KT` is exactly the logic of empty-context `KTProof`s. Consequently
+`SourceStatement` unfolds to
+`∀ L, (∀ φ, L ⊢ ■φ ↔ KT ⊢ φ) → L ⊆ KT`, with the same binder order and no
+extra closure hypothesis.
+
+`CanonicalStatement` is an abbreviation for `SourceStatement`.
+`sourceToCanonical` and `canonicalToSource` are both `id`, and their displayed
+round trip is `Iff.rfl`. The stored declaration text and normalized type bind
+this local proposition to formal-type digest
+`e83fcdbe8f25b7497ae5250dd63e6bae808d257f5d0d8d6ef15acd91ade346d3`.
+
+Formal declarations: `Formula`, `Conj`, `t`, `KProof`, `KTProof`,
+`NormalModalLogic`, `proves`, `KT`, `SourceStatement`, `CanonicalStatement`,
+`sourceToCanonical`, and `canonicalToSource` (`Statement.lean:15-115`).
+
+Output: the exact local representation and proposition to be proved.
+
+Downstream: PU-01, PU-02, and PU-09.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:BEGIN -->
+## PU-01 — Build the formula bijection
+
+Hypotheses: the source formula type from PU-00 and Foundation's vendored
+`LO.Modal.Formula Nat`.
+
+`toFoundation` maps `Atom`, `Falsum`, `Imp`, and `Nec` respectively to
+Foundation's atom, falsum, implication, and box constructors.
+`fromFoundation` performs the reverse recursion. Because neither map changes
+an atom index or inserts a connective, structural induction proves both
+round trips: `fromFoundation (toFoundation φ) = φ` and
+`toFoundation (fromFoundation ψ) = ψ`.
+
+This bijection is important twice: it prevents the semantic theorem from
+changing the source formula carrier, and it lets the last proof step return a
+Foundation KT theorem to the exact source formula with no quotient or
+extensionality argument at the boundary.
+
+Formal declarations: `Adapter.FoundationFormula`, `Adapter.toFoundation`,
+`Adapter.fromFoundation`, `Adapter.from_to`, and `Adapter.to_from`
+(`Proof.lean:16818-16836`).
+
+Output: inverse structural transports between the two formula types.
+
+Downstream: PU-02, PU-03, and PU-08.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:BEGIN -->
+## PU-02 — Transport K and KT derivations in both directions
+
+Hypotheses: the formula bijection from PU-01 and the source Hilbert inductives
+from PU-00.
+
+Forward transport is induction on a source derivation. The three classical
+implication schemata, modus ponens, necessitation, and distribution map to
+Foundation's corresponding K rules. The KT induction additionally maps
+`lift_K` through the K-to-KT weakening and maps `axT` to Foundation's T rule.
+The empty-context side condition is preserved explicitly.
+
+For reverse transport, Foundation represents modal schemata under arbitrary
+substitution. The source definition does not expose substitution as a logic
+record field, so the proof derives it internally. `KProof.subst` and
+`KTProof.subst` are inductions on proof objects; their empty-context versions
+instantiate the K and T schemata. Recursion over
+`LO.Modal.Hilbert.Normal.rec!` then handles axiom instances, modus ponens,
+necessitation, and the three implication rules, producing source K or KT
+proofs. Thus no stronger source interface is assumed.
+
+Formal declarations: `sourceK_to_foundation`, `sourceKT_to_foundation`,
+`Substitution`, `subst`, `KProof.subst`, `KTProof.subst`,
+`toFoundation_subst`, `fromFoundation_subst`, `foundationK_to_source`, and
+`foundationKT_to_source` (`Proof.lean:16838-16998`).
+
+Output: empty-context source K/KT derivations and Foundation K/KT derivations
+can be transported in the directions used by the root proof.
+
+Downstream: PU-03 and PU-08.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:BEGIN -->
+## PU-03 — Reconcile the two boxdot translations
+
+Hypotheses: the transports from PU-01 and PU-02 and an arbitrary modal formula.
+
+`tFoundation` is the Foundation-side image of the source recursion: atoms and
+falsum are fixed, implication is recursive, and a box becomes
+`□(tFoundation φ) ⋏ tFoundation φ`. Induction proves
+`toFoundation (t φ) = tFoundation (toFoundation φ)`.
+
+Foundation's built-in `φᵇ` writes the box case in proposition-first order.
+`foundation_boxdot_iff_tFoundation` proves in K that `φᵇ` is equivalent to
+`tFoundation φ`. The implication case uses congruence. In the box case, box
+congruence transports the induction hypothesis, conjunction congruence joins
+the proposition and boxed parts, and a K theorem commutes the final
+conjunction. Taking the two directions of that equivalence and applying the
+reverse K adapter yields source implications from `fromFoundation (φᵇ)` to
+`t (fromFoundation φ)` and back.
+
+Formal declarations: `tFoundation`, `toFoundation_t`,
+`foundation_boxdot_iff_tFoundation`, `fromFoundation_tFoundation`,
+`foundation_boxdot_to_source`, and `foundation_boxdot_to_source_rev`
+(`Proof.lean:16931-17039`).
+
+Output: a checked K-level bridge between Foundation boxdot membership and the
+source translation appearing in `H`.
+
+Downstream: PU-08.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:BEGIN -->
+## PU-04 — Supply Jeřábek's cluster-expansion infrastructure
+
+Hypotheses: a normal modal logic extending KT, a sound and complete Kripke
+frame class, and closure of that class under the required world duplication.
+
+The vendored Foundation source defines `Frame.twice`: each world is paired
+with a Boolean bit while accessibility is inherited from the original frame.
+Reflexivity and transitivity are preserved, so the KT frame class satisfies
+`JerabekAssumption`. A fresh atom can then be valued differently in the two
+copies without changing the truth of formulas that omit it.
+
+`Formula.flag` selects the fresh atom or its negation. The three private
+lemmas supporting `jerabek_SBDP` establish the flagged boxdot implication,
+lift it to any normal K logic, and relate the boxdot translation of an iterated
+box to a bounded family of translated boxes. Together with the finite global
+consequence interface, these are precisely the tools used in the strong
+boxdot contrapositive.
+
+Formal declarations: `LO.Modal.Kripke.Frame.twice`, preservation instances,
+`FrameClass.KT.JerabekAssumption`, `Formula.flag`,
+`Formula.Kripke.Satisfies.neither_flag`, and
+`jerabek_SBDP.lemma₁` / `lemma₂` / `lemma₃`
+(`Proof.lean:16477-16631`).
+
+Output: typed semantic and syntactic infrastructure for the strong theorem.
+
+Downstream: PU-05 and PU-07.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:BEGIN -->
+## PU-05 — Eliminate boxed subformulas in the finite context
+
+Hypotheses: `φ ∈ L \ L₀`, a fresh atom `q`, a boxed subformula `ψ`, and the
+finite flagged contexts `X₀`, `X₁`, `X`, and `XB` constructed in
+`LO.Modal.jerabek_SBDP`.
+
+The theorem proceeds contrapositively from `L ⊄ L₀`. For every boxed
+subformula it forms both flag choices. Local `Claim1` first lifts the flagged
+semantic lemma to the global-consequence context (`H₁`). Membership in the
+appropriate image context supplies the boxdot theorem for either flag (`H₂`).
+Propositional composition produces a conditional elimination under each flag
+(`H₃`), and the positive and negative cases (`H₄`, `H₅`) discharge the fresh
+atom. The result is `(L, XB.toSet) ⊢ □ψᵇ → ψᵇ`.
+
+No truth-value decision about `q` is added to the logic. Both branches are
+proved and combined classically, which is why the result is usable uniformly
+at every later world.
+
+Formal evidence: local `Claim1` and `H₁` through `H₅` inside
+`LO.Modal.jerabek_SBDP` (`Proof.lean:16651-16684`).
+
+Output: every relevant translated boxed subformula is eliminable in the
+finite global context.
+
+Downstream: PU-06 and PU-07.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:BEGIN -->
+## PU-06 — Translate every selected subformula
+
+Hypotheses: `ψ ∈ φ.subformulas`, the finite context from PU-05, and the
+induction hypotheses for proper subformulas.
+
+Local `Claim2` proves `(L, XB.toSet) ⊢ ψ ↔ ψᵇ` by structural induction.
+Falsum and atoms simplify definitionally. The implication branch composes the
+two induction equivalences by classical propositional reasoning. In the box
+branch, box congruence gives `□ψ ↔ □ψᵇ`; PU-05 supplies
+`□ψᵇ → ψᵇ`, while normal modal reasoning supplies the reverse direction
+needed for `□ψᵇ ↔ ⊡ψᵇ`. Composing those equivalences closes the box case.
+
+Applying the result to the chosen `φ`, and using that `φ` is a theorem of
+`L`, proves its boxdot translation in the finite context. Finite global
+consequence then extracts a concrete finite set `Γ` and box bound `n`.
+
+Formal evidence: local `Claim2`, its four structural branches, the subsequent
+context theorem, and `GlobalConsequence.iff_finite_boxLe_provable`
+(`Proof.lean:16685-16720`).
+
+Output: a bounded finite-context implication whose consequent is `φᵇ`.
+
+Downstream: PU-07.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:BEGIN -->
+## PU-07 — Close the strong theorem with a duplicated countermodel
+
+Hypotheses: the bounded implication from PU-06, the flagged context from
+PU-05, and `φ ∉ L₀`.
+
+The third infrastructure lemma transforms the bounded implication into a
+formula `χ := □^[n](X.conj) → φ` whose boxdot translation belongs to `L`.
+To show `χ ∉ L₀`, completeness gives a model and world refuting `φ`. The proof
+duplicates that model with `Frame.twice` and values only the fresh atom `q`
+according to the Boolean copy. Induction `H2` shows that every subformula of
+`φ` has its old truth value in either copy, because `q` was chosen fresh.
+
+Each member of the flagged context is true in the duplicated model: if its
+consequent is already true there is nothing to prove; otherwise the copy with
+the required flag forces the antecedent to fail. Hence the iterated boxed
+context is true, while `H2` preserves the falsity of `φ` at the selected world.
+The world refutes `χ`. Soundness therefore excludes `χ` from `L₀`, providing
+a member of the boxdot preimage outside `L₀` and completing the contrapositive.
+
+Formal evidence: the local `χ`, `M₂`, `H2`, context-validity argument, and
+countermodel conclusion in `LO.Modal.jerabek_SBDP`, followed by
+`jerabek_BDP` and `KT.BDP` (`Proof.lean:16721-16801`).
+
+Output: `LO.Modal.jerabek_SBDP`, the strong boxdot property used at the source
+boundary.
+
+Downstream: PU-08.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-08:BEGIN -->
+## PU-08 — Specialize the strong theorem to the frozen logic
+
+Hypotheses: source `L`, the unchanged frozen premise `H`, and the transports
+from PU-01 through PU-03.
+
+`sourceLogic L` defines a Foundation logic by membership of
+`fromFoundation φ` in `L`. `sourceLogic_mem_of_K` and `sourceLogic_K` derive
+the required normal K instance solely from the source fields `extK`, `mp`, and
+`nec`. Now take a formula in this logic's boxdot preimage. Its translated
+Foundation theorem maps through PU-03 to a source proof of
+`t (fromFoundation φ)`; the forward direction of `H` gives a source KT proof,
+and PU-02 transports that proof to Foundation KT. This proves
+`source_boxdot_preimage_subset`.
+
+Specializing `LO.Modal.jerabek_SBDP` to `L₀ := LO.Modal.KT`, its KT frame
+class, and `sourceLogic L` yields `sourceLogic L ⊆ LO.Modal.KT`. Given
+`φ ∈ L`, PU-01 puts `toFoundation φ` in `sourceLogic L`; the inclusion makes
+it a Foundation KT theorem; PU-02 returns a source `KTProof ∅ φ`.
+
+Formal declarations: `sourceLogic`, `sourceLogic_mem_of_K`, `sourceLogic_K`,
+`source_boxdot_preimage_subset`, and `boxdotConjecture_proof`
+(`Proof.lean:17041-17119`).
+
+Output: `Adapter.boxdotConjecture_proof L H : L ⊆ KT` with exactly the frozen
+premises.
+
+Downstream: PU-09.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-08:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-09:BEGIN -->
+## PU-09 — Expose and audit the exact root
+
+Hypotheses: none outside the quantifiers and premise already stored in
+`SourceStatement`.
+
+`boxdotConjectureStatement` introduces `L` and `H` and applies the proof from
+PU-08. `theorem_complete` is a stable theorem of `SourceStatement`, not a
+second reformulation. `Audit.lean` checks the root as both `SourceStatement`
+and `CanonicalStatement`, applies it to an arbitrary source `L` and `H`, and
+prints dependencies for both public names. A compatibility namespace exposes
+the same proof under the shorter historical path without changing the root.
+
+The cold trust-zero audit reports exactly `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and
+`Quot.sound`. It reports no `sorryAx`; static inspection also finds no local
+placeholder, unsafe declaration, claim-specific axiom, or bodyless oracle.
+
+Formal declarations: `boxdotConjectureStatement`, `theorem_complete`, and the
+typed checks in `Audit.lean` (`Proof.lean:17123-17129`; `Audit.lean:1-46`).
+
+Output:
+`AwesomeTheorems.Stage5.Theorems.S5_CLM_00003489.theorem_complete : SourceStatement`.
+
+Downstream: terminal provisional handoff for independent canonical Master
+review.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-09:END -->
